// WQL — A `u64` AGGREGATE ARGUMENT IS REFUSED WITH THE SAME GROUND AS EVERY
// OTHER SITE THAT NORMALIZES INTO THE EL's i64 TOWER.
//
// `wql_arith_wide_int_fail` pins this refusal for an arithmetic OPERAND and
// `wql_udf_wide_int_ret_fail` for a UDF RESULT; the aggregate argument is the
// third site the same `as i64` is reached from, and it had no check at all.
// MEASURED at the parent commit (6440e565), this file:
//   <metaprog-blob-subst>: error [fn q_run]: call to 'el_add' arg 2:
//     expected i64, got u64
// and `min`/`max` over the same column: `let '__v': type mismatch — expected
// i64, got u64`.
//
// ⚠ THE GROUND IS SHARED, NOT RE-WORDED. `el_order_why` renders it once, for
// all three sites; a second copy of those sentences is a second thing to drift.
// The same applies to `usize`, `u128` and `i128`.
//
// ⚠ AND THE UNIT OF THAT SHARING WAS WRONG UNTIL 2026-08-05, WHICH IS WHY THE
// `.expected` BESIDE THIS FILE MOVED. The sentence was shared with the SORT
// site as well, and it ended by asserting that "an `order by` over such a key
// ranks the largest values below zero … so no plan decision downstream can
// detect it". This tier — the one that prints the string — compiles
// `order by s.v` over a `u64` column and ranks 5, 2^63, 2^64-1 CORRECTLY: the
// key vector holds the key's own type and compares in it, so the clause
// described a static code path retired when key vectors stopped being
// `Vec<i64>`. It was true only of the DYNAMIC tier, which never renders this
// string. `el_ord_why_code` now takes the QUESTION it is asked from
// (`EL_WHY_NORM` here, `EL_WHY_SORT` at `el_cmp_why`); the type-level fact and
// the remedy are unchanged and still shared, and the clause between them is the
// position's. `tests/logos/pass/wql_el_ord_why_split` is the canary over the
// duplication that split forced, and
// `tests/logos/pass/wql_tier_capability_disagreement` pins the `order by`
// acceptance that made the old clause false.
//
// ⚠ THE REFUSAL ITSELF IS UNCHANGED, AND ITS ANTECEDENT MOVED UNDER IT WITHOUT
// TOUCHING IT — which is the strongest thing this file says. The check no longer
// asks `el_host_carries` ("does the HOST widen this into the accumulator
// unwritten") but `el_agg_arg_reaches` (`ElTy.fits` + `el_norm_cast`, the
// question every other EL operand position asks), and `compute_agg_bodies` now
// WRITES the cast. That admitted `isize` — see `tests/logos/pass/
// wql_agg_isize_arg_e2e`, which was this file's sibling in `fail/` until
// 2026-08-05. It does NOT admit `u64`: `fits` is false, the cast REINTERPRETS,
// and the refusal below rests on the value rather than on what the backend will
// carry. A widening that reached this cell would have shipped a wrong number,
// so this test staying RED across that change is the point of it.
//
// Only the ground it prints moved before that. A control revert of the renderer
// alone leaves this test red on its `.expected` and every other
// `wql_agg_*_arg_fail` green, which is what says the two are separable.
//
// ⚠ AND `avg` OVER THIS COLUMN STILL COMPILES, which is not an inconsistency:
// its fold emitter writes `((s.v) as f64)` itself, so the argument never meets
// the i64 tower. The check is skipped exactly there (`agg_cast`), because the
// question is whether the value reaches the ACCUMULATOR — and for a NUMERIC
// argument it does.
//
// ⚠ THAT LAST WORD WAS MISSING, AND ITS ABSENCE WAS A DEFECT. The sentence
// above justified the exemption for every shape `agg_cast` covered, and
// `agg_cast` was keyed on the argument being NOT A FLOAT rather than on its
// being numeric — so `avg` over a `str` column was exempted by this same
// reasoning, and it is false there: `(s.v) as f64` over a `str` is not a
// conversion, it is the "unsupported cast" mlir-gen printed while returning 0
// and writing the object file. The claim is sound for `u64` and every other
// integer width; it was being used far outside them. See
// `wql_agg_avg_str_arg_fail` for the refusal and `compute_agg_reprs` for the
// fabricated argument class that was the root.
package test;

use logos.std.wql.wql;
use logos.mem.collections.vec;

struct Rec { pub k: i64, pub v: u64 }

pub deem totals(src: &[Rec]) { from src s
      group by s.k
      aggregate t = sum(s.v)
      select t : i64 }

fn main() -> i32 { return 0i32; }
